      "id": 1,
      "text": "Pinckneyville was organized on May 17, 1857, and immediately became the county seat of Perry County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinckneyville%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name honors Charles Cotesworth Pinckney, an early American diplomat and presidential candidate.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinckneyville%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The original 1834 courthouse was a simple log structure; a larger jail built in 1871 now houses the Perry County Jail Museum.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinckneyville%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Pinckneyville is home to the Pinckneyville Power Plant, a combustion turbine generator run by Ameren.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinckneyville%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Visitors can explore Pinckneyville’s past at the Perry County Jail Museum and the local library, both key research spots.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
